The daemon list belongs to the machine, not to the program
=========================================================

The page shipped with one particular computer's services baked into it: a speech-to-text unit, a voice
unit, an embedding index, a model server and a vision unit, each with the port that machine happened to
use. On the machine those came from, that is a useful page. Anywhere else it is a list of somebody else's
daemons, with somebody else's port numbers, presented as if they were yours.

It reads `<data_dir>/daemons.json` now:

```json
[{"name":"Speech to text","unit":"stt.service","port":8080,"what":"dictation and transcription"}]
```

With no file, the single row is `ydotoold` — the one service this app can name without guessing, because
input synthesis does not work without it. The page says where the list comes from, so an empty page is an
instruction rather than a mystery.

